Q: How to use std::format with a base template for all derived classes A: <p>As you seem to have noticed, partial specialization matching requires an exact match. Your derived types are not <em>exactly</em> <code>Base&lt;T, D&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>Since all derived classes share the same pattern, you could use <code>using</code> instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>using DerivedFp = Base&lt;double&gt;;
using DerivedInt = Base&lt;int&gt;;
using DerivedBool = Base&lt;bool&gt;;
</code></pre>
<p>I suspect that the above will not be satisfactory for long and if you actually need classes derived from <code>Base&lt;T, D&gt;</code>, specialize <code>std::formatter</code> for them all by inheriting from the <code>std::formatter&lt;Base&lt;T, D&gt;&gt;</code> you've already defined:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>// this function template can deduce the template parameters when given
// a derived type:
template &lt;class U, unsigned int E&gt;
auto Base_extractor(const Base&lt;U, E&gt;&amp;) -&gt; Base&lt;U, E&gt;;  // never defined

// std::formatter specializations for all types derived from Base&lt;T, D&gt;
template &lt;class T&gt;
    requires requires(const T&amp; t) { Base_extractor(t); } // constrain it
struct std::formatter&lt;T&gt;        // derive from the Base&lt;T, D&gt; formatter:
    : std::formatter&lt;decltype(Base_extractor(std::declval&lt;const T&amp;&gt;()))&gt; {};
</code></pre>
<p>An alternative which may be easier to read but does the same thing:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class U, unsigned int E&gt;
auto Base_extractor(const Base&lt;U, E&gt;&amp;) -&gt; std::formatter&lt;Base&lt;U, E&gt;&gt;;

template &lt;class T&gt;
    requires requires(const T&amp; t) { Base_extractor(t); }
struct std::formatter&lt;T&gt;
    : decltype(Base_extractor(std::declval&lt;const T&amp;&gt;())){};
</code></pre>
